#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055} :activity/id #uuid "4918d79b-bb18-5ebc-b1d6-c9cd4c5a9bf1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c717541104571d6dce45dc88e30c4ff2dd42471d2348fe05b7d91ce395adae4.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p),\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX  \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p,$ we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}].$\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4cf166b-07f1-5e2e-9b9a-8147d42ce348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9526 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ce0e2df-ecaa-5cb0-b322-b2b78e5030ce" :step/math-academy-id 187404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189501 :tutorial/content "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n\n- $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5,$ *and*\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\n*Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right).\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5,$\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1addf4e-37b8-58a0-827e-70f370944f92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9527 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Sample Distribution Model"} :step/id #uuid "32038366-e144-50bf-8a9e-d6ae30f4a820" :step/math-academy-id 187405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188193 :question/id #uuid "ddfa9789-7019-5939-b675-bcef59e5ef4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552503}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "672cee66-8553-5cd8-8135-323a5632bbce" :step/math-academy-id 187406 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188194 :question/id #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" :question/math-academy-id "e-15152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552449}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "4c971f78-cc05-5002-90c0-b319d1ef39e2" :step/math-academy-id 187409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188195 :question/id #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" :question/math-academy-id "e-15153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552426}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"} :step/id #uuid "69641840-a7d8-502b-b5fc-162ab4534b75" :step/math-academy-id 187412}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188515}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513003} :activity/id #uuid "b137b7a5-4e68-530a-a72a-5c5c10f189b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512261} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512327} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512400} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147386" :question/problem "A fitness center has $1600$ members, $480$ of which play tennis. If the center randomly samples $60$ members, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ | $\\,0.9429\\,$ | $\\,0.9441\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ | $\\,0.9545\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ | $\\,0.9633\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{480}{1600}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & >\\frac{24}{60}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.69)=0.9545.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}) & =1-Φ(1.69) \\\\  & =0.0455.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.66)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512316} {:answer/id #uuid "873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512413} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} {:answer/id #uuid "e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.53)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab3a112-abbd-5b74-bf90-3997e193c1b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0faf39ad-2709-5ec7-94d0-99312772d48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146155" :question/problem "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.38.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.38)=76>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.38)=124>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $80$ families in the sample own a pet, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}>\\frac{80}{200}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.38}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.58) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8117" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512113} {:answer/id #uuid "1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7582" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512134} {:answer/id #uuid "27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512187} {:answer/id #uuid "473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147211" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $70%$ but less than $80%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9671\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} {:answer/id #uuid "71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.29)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512368} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512429} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512562} {:answer/id #uuid "e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} :answer-field/id #uuid "99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146974" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513021}}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512091}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516879} :activity/id #uuid "695f57f1-7bc3-58a6-8971-b31a35d4ca12" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.34)-Φ(-1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516227} {:answer/id #uuid "6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.28)-Φ(-1.28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516322} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(0.98)-Φ(-0.98)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516546} {:answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(0.72)-Φ(-0.72)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7552ed-0a05-5263-8474-d257c2707a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b040f12-bfb6-5271-9a10-66bca5c20f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-146163" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=100(0.6)=60>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=100(1-0.6)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=100$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65) & =P(\\frac{0.55-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.65-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.02\\le Z\\le 1.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.02)-P(Z\\le -1.02) \\\\  & =Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "119b64cb-4a4b-5784-891f-c5aa424cbe05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516439} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516443} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516455} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516579} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-147070" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $36%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $28%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.36.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.36)=34.56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.36)=61.44>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,\\frac{0.36(1-0.36)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.28-0.36}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.63) & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =Φ(-1.63) \\\\  & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7b7ccdb8-8a3b-5849-930e-28332d5bed17"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.19)-Φ(-1.19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516124} {:answer/id #uuid "076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(0.69)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516127} {:answer/id #uuid "54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(2.26)-Φ(-2.26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516290} {:answer/id #uuid "551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.36)-Φ(-1.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516292} {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146937" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $60%$ and $80%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2bec5e3-fc63-5c73-984d-80b18cc96044" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516923}}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516098}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552594} :activity/id #uuid "591a24fb-911f-57d6-83d8-0a9faf369ec8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eac5ebc-2001-5939-bb01-a23db90d16ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8993" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551777} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7e5d5d-eaba-5b52-abb2-c07c5cabfd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8966" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551845} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3f1472-3696-5c97-aba0-3dd00565cf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552073} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23aab7-df07-55de-bc6b-69004196684a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9399" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552116} {:answer/id #uuid "f904190a-a9e5-53be-ad83-84813e7c98d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552116} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a3f931d-2db0-5acf-8977-4c0279c7518b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c142fbc-f51c-5174-b26c-467ddea21c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-248223" :question/problem "A newspaper in a town has $100\\,000$ subscribers, and $56\\,000$ of them have a digital subscription. If the newspaper randomly samples $224$ subscribers, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $112$ and $140$ subscribers (inclusive) in the sample have a digital subscription. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0359\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0351\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0344\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9616\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9693\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9756\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=224,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{56\\,000}{100\\,000}=0.56.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=224(0.56)=125.44>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=224(1-0.56)=98.56>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=224$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,\\frac{0.56(1-0.56)}{224}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,0.0011).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $112$ and $140$ of subscribers in the sample have a digital subscription, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{112}{224}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{140}{224} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.625-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.81\\le Z\\le 1.96) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.96)-P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.96)=0.9750,\\,Φ(-1.81)=0.0351.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.9750-0.0351 \\\\  & =0.9399.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e6af469-3c48-502f-905e-006eddadc5f7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e47d81a-96ba-562d-8953-23216385e09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.85)-Φ(-1.85)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551729} {:answer/id #uuid "56ccccfb-ea6a-5a3b-82bb-275b7a08df9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551872} {:answer/id #uuid "75b61603-62d8-520b-abdf-6ee921cee91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551943} {:answer/id #uuid "93e4abf7-ab00-51bb-883e-429ea9708a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552023} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f8810-daf6-5c36-bcdb-37a7726ca2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552084} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bcc9c4-cdd4-55e6-a5b0-8d30dd034164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "690b1c72-fb42-5766-9459-80c219f09698" :question/math-academy-id "q-146954" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population where $30%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $40%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2771376d-b7ef-5e13-be47-2d41a0e0b7ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1202bf1a-f706-5bcf-af3a-f876dc090d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551703} {:answer/id #uuid "1952d9ec-6286-5a4c-9e84-d0c4ccd6aaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551717} {:answer/id #uuid "442774be-a20d-58a2-b4e2-2c8322da6b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551830} {:answer/id #uuid "978d6838-a763-599d-98a7-3c0d59e3b265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552032} {:answer/id #uuid "9f095ae4-fbdf-5a24-8f87-ad73d95fa0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551717} :answer-field/id #uuid "c524fbfd-0dc2-5174-8d0f-5797b1138e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c3d4715f-79a5-5718-adbc-5959190456a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146831" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ students from a class, where $60%$ of the students in the school have an IQ greater than $105.$\n\nII. A sample of $60$ paid TV customers, where $80%$ of the customers watch TV more than $8$ hours per week.\n\nIII. A sample of $40$ players in a team, $90%$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.6) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60(0.8) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.8) \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b0aa4ac-1003-50f7-8beb-0e192289f5b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8e04cb-b825-58db-8ad8-b03e2d067c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-1.91)-Φ(0.15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551693} {:answer/id #uuid "334e3f84-c380-5cf4-8954-e5d170c7537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.91)-Φ(1.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551786} {:answer/id #uuid "34c55053-9c11-5d1e-8f12-735d70f72013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.07)-Φ(0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551789} {:answer/id #uuid "7a889bb5-4965-5d5d-b3d9-3edc871df271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.35)-Φ(0.12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551955} {:answer/id #uuid "9559b7c5-7596-50f4-809d-c63614192c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(0.91)-Φ(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb91e50-0e51-565e-98f0-4e7b44b96808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b794abe-e844-5b01-84a2-a65b6d920bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-147484" :question/problem "A call center manager knows that $40%$ of the calls are complaints. If the manager randomly samples $80$ calls, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ of them are complaints. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.4.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.4)=32>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.4)=48>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,0.0030).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ calls are complaints, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{80}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{36}{80} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.45) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}<Z<\\frac{0.45-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<0.91) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.91)-P(Z<0.00) \\\\  & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0.00)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45) & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84cbff4c-5a75-5a51-933a-9d7902aaaddf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8383fc80-11b0-5ef7-a4ff-98907dac9895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186385} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dfffd9-756c-54ff-9fad-72bb1b97c8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186386} {:answer/id #uuid "3f023834-d3ab-5a26-93f8-ddbe345ca7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9747" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186387} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed7bd2-bde0-5e7d-9d0e-d101acece082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186388} {:answer/id #uuid "788ac932-3ccb-52cf-888f-ee298f7621e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186186388} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4a4235-f096-5b72-a48d-8a38aff78371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbed22eb-e3d1-5c23-b166-31d76c217f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146165" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $42%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $35%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0735\\,$ | $\\,0.0721\\,$ | $\\,0.0708\\,$ | $\\,0.0694\\,$ | $\\,0.0681\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ | $\\,0.0869\\,$ | $\\,0.0853\\,$ | $\\,0.0838\\,$ | $\\,0.0823\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ | $\\,0.1038\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.42.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.42)=33.6>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.42)=46.4>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,0.003\\,045).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.35-0.42}{\\sqrt{0.003\\,045}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.27).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.27) & =0.1020.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35) & =1-Φ(-1.27) \\\\  & =1-0.1020 \\\\  & =0.8980.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b592bdb7-ec73-5da1-b03e-7cd731635534" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552557}}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551653}]}
